---

> We're all hurtling towards death, yet here we are for the moment, alive.

Waking, eating, interacting, sleeping, repeating. Everyday we seek the 'answer' to it all but its not until the absurdity of the world enters our lives that we realize the impermanence of it. Caden spends decades perfecting his work and trying to find the meaning of it all, but he never lived life itself. It's not until death that he realizes the truth. A poetic but tragic end.

> They say there is no fate, but there is: it's what you create. Even though the world goes on for eons and eons, you are here for a fraction of a fraction of a second. Most of your time is spent being dead or not yet born. But while alive, you wait in vain, wasting years, for a phone call or a letter or a look from someone or something to make it all right. And it never comes or it seems to but doesn't really. And so you spend your time in vague regret or vaguer hope for something good to come along. Something to make you feel connected, to make you feel whole, to make you feel loved.
